Beyond Substance: A Primitive Ontology from Distinction to Emergent Spacetime

ouadi Maakoul 


Abstract

We propose a minimal set of ontologically primitive ingredients from which all physical structure can, in principle, emerge. Rejecting substance-based metaphysics, we argue that the universe is fundamentally a network of distinctions governed by lawful relations and ordered transitions. The framework is anchored in four non-eliminable primitives: **Plurality** (the existence of at least one difference), **Configuration** (the selection of a state across a plurality), **Lawful Relation** (invariant constraints on configurations), and **Transition** (the capacity for ordered change). We argue these four are jointly necessary and, within explicitly stated scope restrictions, together provide a sufficient conceptual foundation from which spacetime, particles, and observers may be derived without circular reliance on pre-existing matter, metric, or consciousness.

The argument is anchored in concrete and current physics: Spencer-Brown's calculus of indications [1]; Landauer's principle [2]; the holographic entanglement-geometry correspondence with critical de Sitter caveats, including the new bilayer and static-patch holography proposals [3,4,25]; the Page–Wootters mechanism and its recent extension to full 3+1 quantum spacetime [5,6,7,44,45]; the updated ontology of Relational Quantum Mechanics [8,2-1]; and Esfeld's 2025 synthesis of primitive ontology with structural realism [24-1]. We critically examine the Mathematical Universe Hypothesis, adopt mathematical consistency as necessary but not sufficient for physical instantiation [9,10], and acknowledge the bootstrapping problem of the first distinction [11] and clock-accuracy limitations [12]. The resulting ontology places relations — not objects — at the deepest level of reality.

---

## 1. Introduction

What must exist for existence itself to be possible? This ancient question has been radically sharpened by recent developments across quantum gravity, quantum information theory, and the foundations of quantum mechanics, all of which increasingly converge on the view that the familiar ontological categories — matter, space, and time — are emergent from a deeper, relational layer. The purpose of this paper is to construct a defensible minimal ontology: a set of irreducible primitives that are both necessary and provide a conceptual pathway toward sufficiency for generating the known features of physical reality, without circular reliance on higher-level entities.

Our starting point is the observation that a universe without distinguishable states is operationally identical to no universe at all. From this seed, we unfold a chain of reasoning leading to four interdependent primitives. These primitives are not invented; they are the minimum presuppositions of any physical description whatsoever. The structure that emerges is one of pure relationality: things are replaced by differences interacting through consistent structure. As the most recent formulation of Relational Quantum Dynamics puts it, quantum mechanics, spacetime, and observers are no longer separate domains but aspects of one interconnected informational web. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.05979)

The paper is structured as follows. Section 2 introduces the first primitive — distinction — formalised via Spencer-Brown's calculus while honestly framing the unresolved bootstrapping problem. Section 3 addresses how distinction gives rise to information and connects it to physics via Landauer's principle and the entanglement-geometry correspondence. Section 4 discusses lawful relations, grounding the ontology in Esfeld's 2025 synthesis of primitive ontology and structural realism. Section 5 derives dynamics from the Page–Wootters mechanism, now significantly strengthened by Favalli's 2025 derivation of relativistic wave equations from constraints alone. Section 6 addresses recursive structure formation. Section 7 confronts mathematical intelligibility. Section 8 reformulates observability relationally. Section 9 synthesises the primitives into a dependency graph, and Section 10 concludes.

