## 4. Lawful Relation: Structural Realism Reforged

Information alone — even entangled — is insufficient to produce a world with persistent objects and predictable evolution. There must be stable, repeatable relations between configurations. A mere set of random transitions would yield noise. For structure to persist, transitions must be invariant under certain transformations. This is the core intuition behind ontic structural realism (OSR).

**The debate and its current state.** Proponents of OSR claim that it is a "naturalistic" metaphysics, arguing that metaphysical views that take objects and/or properties as ontologically primitive are undermined by contemporary physics. [ResearchGate](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/248526492_What_is_ontic_structural_realism) The debate has sharpened considerably in recent years. A radical structuralist version is possible, though the debate regarding spacetime OSR typically focuses on general relativity; notably, the hole argument is often invoked as grounds for rejecting the attribution of primitive identity to spacetime points. [Springer](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11229-025-05120-5)

The most important recent development for our purposes is Esfeld's 2025 synthesis. His essay starts from a primitive ontology that replaces intrinsic properties with structures in the sense of relations, with distance being the natural, world-making relation that individuates [PhilSci-Archive](https://philsci-archive.pitt.edu/27732/) physical entities [24]. This directly addresses the classic "relations without relata" objection: rather than demanding pre-given objects that *have* relations, Esfeld demonstrates that the distance relations themselves *individuate* the particles, so that their numerical plurality is not primitive, but derives from the relations in which they stand. [PhilSci-Archive](https://philsci-archive.pitt.edu/27732/2/PO-Laws-ProjectNomos-2025.pdf) This is a substantially more rigorous grounding for our third primitive than was available when the Esfeld-Lam 2008 paper was the primary reference.

A further 2025 contribution proposes the Universal Theory of Structure: ontic structural realism holds that structure is ontologically fundamental, yet it has lacked a precise metaphysical account of structure. [PhilSci-Archive](https://philsci-archive.pitt.edu/27806/) This ongoing programme to formalise what "structure" means at the deepest level is directly continuous with our framework.

We adopt the moderate structural realist position: **Lawful Relation** is the requirement of invariant correlations, from which effective physical law can emerge. The ontology is one of patterns of constrained difference.

