## 5. Transition: Dynamics and the Emergence of Time

If the world were a single, static block of distinctions and relations, no process, no cause, no experience would occur. A minimal ontology requires the capacity for ordered change. This is the fourth primitive: **Transition**.

Transition does not necessitate a pre-existing temporal dimension. The Page–Wootters (PW) mechanism [5,6] shows how time can emerge from quantum entanglement between subsystems under a total Hamiltonian constraint. The universe is described by a stationary state $|\Psi\rangle\!\rangle \in \mathcal{H}_C \otimes \mathcal{H}_S$, where $\mathcal{H}_C$ is a "clock" system and $\mathcal{H}_S$ is the rest. The total Hamiltonian is a constraint:

$$(H_C \otimes I_S + I_C \otimes H_S)|\Psi\rangle\!\rangle = 0.$$

No external time parameter exists. Yet an internal time can be defined by choosing a suitable clock observable, and it follows that the relative state of the system satisfies precisely the Schrödinger equation. This mechanism was experimentally illustrated with entangled photon pairs [6].

**Crucial 2025 strengthening: full spacetime from constraints.** The most significant recent advance bearing directly on this primitive is Favalli's 2025 paper [7, 41–43]. In previous works, Favalli showed that both time and space can emerge from entanglement within a globally constrained quantum Universe, with no background coordinates. By extending the Page–Wootters quantum time formalism to include both quantum clocks and rods, and imposing global constraints on total energy and momentum, a fully relational model of quantum spacetime was constructed. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.12698) The 2025 paper takes a decisive further step: working in 1+1 dimensions, the standard wave equations governing quantum particles — the Schrödinger, Klein–Gordon and Dirac equations — emerge naturally from this framework, with the solutions derived directly from the constraints, without assuming any external spacetime structure. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2508.12698) This is a landmark result: the entire kinematic architecture of quantum field theory can now be grounded in constraint-based relational entanglement, exactly as our primitive of Transition requires.

**3+1 dimensional spacetime emergence.** The earlier Favalli–Smerzi model [44] had already generalised the PW formalism: considering a global quantum system satisfying a double constraint on both total energy and total momentum, it provides a model of 3+1 dimensional, non-relativistic, quantum spacetime emerging from entanglement among different subsystems in a globally "timeless" and "positionless" Universe. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2207.01005) The Springer monograph [12-1] extends this to demonstrate agreement with the Schwarzschild solution for time dilation, confirming the framework's consistency with classical general relativity in the appropriate limit.

**Clock accuracy limitation.** A known technical limitation of any quantum clock model is the Salecker–Wigner–Peres bound, formalised by Woods, Silva, and Oppenheim [12]. The PW mechanism does not deliver a perfect, cost-free temporal parameter; it delivers an emergent approximation whose fidelity degrades for finite-sized clocks. We flag this as an open refinement rather than a fatal objection.

We ground Transition in this physics: change is the actualisation of ordered correlations between subsystems, with no temporal container. Time is a measure of correlation, not a fundamental stage.

