## 6. Recursive Structure Formation and Emergence

Given the four primitives — Plurality, Configuration, Lawful Relation, and Transition — complex structure can form through recursion and feedback. Transitions that feed back into configurations generate hierarchy: from elementary constraints emerge particles, atoms, chemistry, and ultimately life.

Mathematically, this is captured by renormalisation group flow and cellular automata. Simple update rules can produce arbitrarily complex patterns. We adopt the view that such complexity is weakly emergent: reducible in principle to the underlying primitives, but not practically predictable without simulation. The question of whether genuinely novel causal powers appear at certain levels — especially concerning consciousness — remains philosophically debated and is beyond the scope of this paper. Our framework is compatible with weak emergence for all physical properties. The critical point is that no additional physical primitives are needed to accommodate the observed self-organisation of the cosmos.

