## 8. Relational Observability Without Verificationism

Some ontological proposals include "observability" as a primitive, arguing that an unobservable universe is indistinguishable from non-existence. This is a verificationist claim. A more rigorous foundation is Relational Quantum Mechanics (RQM), which holds that physical facts are always relative to an interacting system, but that any physical interaction qualifies as an observation — no consciousness is required [8].

**Recent advances in RQM ontology.** A 2025 paper in *Foundations of Physics* constructs an updated ontology explicitly grounded in RQM: it aims to construct an ontology grounded in the interpretation of Relational Quantum Mechanics, which serves as a robust framework for a realist interpretation of quantum mechanics without the need for additional theoretical constructs. [Springer](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10701-025-00867-w) Fano and Sanchioni further show that this ontological perspective provides new insights into quantum paradoxes [2-1]. The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy's Fall 2025 edition on RQM [4] documents the considerable maturation of this programme, including a recent alternative that reduces perspectivalism to relativism, assuming that values of variables are relative, but the fact that a variable has a value relative to a system is an absolute fact, which by itself is not relative to anything. [Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy](https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2025/entries/qm-relational/) This "tamed" version of RQM substantially weakens the Frauchiger–Renner objection's force by anchoring at least one level of fact in non-perspectival terms.

More recently, Adlam and Rovelli define a quantum event as arising in an interaction between two systems such that the values of some physical variables of one system become definite relative to another system [Lse](https://philosophyofphysics.lse.ac.uk/articles/209/files/68c7ed7fc30f8.pdf) [7-1]. This event-based ontology directly operationalises what we mean by Configuration becoming actual through Transition.

Outstanding challenges remain. Calosi and Riedel (2024) have noted the problem of making the notion of interaction precise as one of RQM's open problems [Lse](https://philosophyofphysics.lse.ac.uk/articles/209/files/68c7ed7fc30f8.pdf) , and Faglia (2025) argues that RQM has not yet fully resolved the measurement problem without a more precise specification of when events occur [7-1]. We acknowledge these as genuine open frontiers.

Thus, observability is not an additional primitive; it is an emergent property of the existing primitives under real-world interactions.

