## 9. Synthesis: The Dependency Graph

The four primitives stand in a logical and generative order:

1. **Plurality** — the existence of at least one distinction.
2. **Configuration** — the selection of a specific state across the plurality.
3. **Lawful Relation** — invariant constraints on how configurations can differ.
4. **Transition** — the ordering of configurations according to those constraints.

From these, everything else emerges, at least in principle:

$$\text{Plurality} \;\rightarrow\; \text{Configuration} \;\rightarrow\; \text{Lawful Relation} \;\rightarrow\; \text{Transition}$$

$$\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \Downarrow$$

$$\text{Spacetime (entanglement geometry, constraint-derived)} \;\rightarrow\; \text{Particles, forces} \;\rightarrow\; \text{Complexity, observers}$$

The arrow to spacetime is now grounded in three converging research programmes: (i) the Ryu–Takayanagi formula in AdS/CFT [3]; (ii) new holographic entanglement entropy proposals extending the geometry-entanglement link into de Sitter space [25, 31, 32]; and (iii) Favalli's constraint-based derivation of full 3+1 quantum spacetime and relativistic wave equations without background coordinates [7, 41–45]. These together constitute not merely a motivating analogy but a partially constructed derivation.

The dependency map remains a conceptual roadmap, not a claim of accomplished full reduction. The frontiers are clearly marked: the bootstrapping problem of the first distinction, the de Sitter holography programme, the selection of specific physical laws, finite clock accuracy, the ontological precision of RQM interaction events, and the emergence of observers.

---

## 10. Conclusion

We have argued that the physical universe requires no primary substance. Its foundation consists of four relational primitives: Plurality, Configuration, Lawful Relation, and Transition. This framework stands up to scrutiny from mathematical logic, information theory, thermodynamics, quantum gravity, and the philosophy of physics. It avoids the pitfalls of both naive materialism and Platonism, and it makes explicit contact with the most current research programmes.

Since the original version of this paper, the empirical and theoretical basis of the framework has been substantially strengthened. The Page–Wootters programme has been extended to derive full 3+1 dimensional spacetime, relativistic wave equations, and gravitational time dilation from entanglement and constraints alone, without any background spacetime structure [7, 41–45]. New holographic proposals have begun to close the gap between the AdS result and the de Sitter universe we inhabit [25, 31, 32]. Esfeld's 2025 synthesis has provided the primitive ontology programme with its most rigorous structural realist foundation to date [24]. And the ontology of RQM has been sharpened into a precise event-based framework that operationalises how Configuration becomes actual through Transition [2-1, 5-1].

The open boundaries remain real and important: the bootstrapping problem of the first distinction; the incomplete de Sitter holography programme; the unresolved selection of specific physical laws; finite clock accuracy; the measurement problem in RQM; and the schematic nature of observer emergence. A minimal ontology should not pretend to have all the answers; it should provide the most economical consistent foundation and clearly mark the frontiers where deeper principles are still needed.

The deepest shift is ontological: the world is not a collection of nouns but a web of verbs. It is not made of things, but of differences interacting through consistent structure. By embracing this relational foundation with due modesty and rigour, and by anchoring it in the best current physics, we open a path toward a truly minimal and unified understanding of existence.

